===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-|nolinkhead=1}}

# Adjoining and connected (to a bedroom or bathroom).
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2016-11-10|author=Teresa Stoppani|author2=Giorgio Ponzo|author3=George Themistokleous|title=This Thing Called Theory|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9781315406244
|text={{...}} the boudoir of Mme de Saint-Ange. This architectural type, a small, furnished and ornate feminine aristocratic space dedicated to bathing and dressing, located '''en suite''' with bedroom and salon or dining room, and used by the lady of the house to withdraw or to receive private guests for intimate conversation (or more), is the setting for both exclusion and excess, chosen by de Sade{{nb...}}}}
# So as to form cohesively themed or [[matching]] set.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Stuart W. Pyhrr|author2=Filippo Negroli|author3=José-A. Godoy|author4=Metropolitan Museum of Art (New York, N.Y.)|title=Heroic Armor of the Italian Renaissance: Filippo Negroli and His Contemporaries|publisher=Metropolitan Museum of Art|isbn=9780870998720|page=262
|text=The burgonet was decorated '''en suite''', the sides of the bowl embossed with acanthus scrolls flanking a central palmette and with swagged ribbons at the peak and nape, the hinged cheekpieces embossed in the center with a leafy mask with foliate diadem{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Ewart Oakeshott|title=European Weapons and Armour: From the Renaissance to the Industrial Revolution|publisher=Boydell Press|isbn=9781843837206|page=249
|text=The grips of these hilts {{...}} were of solid metal which was decorated '''''en suite''''' with the rest of the hilt.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-04-29|author=J. Raven|title=Lost Mansions: Essays on the Destruction of the Country House|publisher=Springer|isbn=9781137520777
|text={{...}} we can see that each room was decorated '''en suite''', with wallpapers and drapery in matching colours.}}

